(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-anti-infectives-quiz"]={"title":"Ocular Delivery & Anti-infectives Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the stated advantages of the topical route?","choices":["Convenient, economical and relatively safe","Sustained release without repeated dosing","Reliable delivery to the posterior segment","Avoidance of any systemic exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That combination is why almost every ophthalmic drug is a drop or an ointment. The price is compliance, surface toxicity and systemic absorption by the nasolacrimal route. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which route is described for anterior segment surgery and infection, with prompt absorption and a relatively short duration?","choices":["Topical instillation","Intracameral injection","Retrobulbar injection","Oral administration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prompt onset, used in anterior segment surgery and infection, but its effect is relatively short-lived and it carries corneal and intraocular toxicity. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Azithromycin solution is dosed twice daily while erythromycin ointment may be needed four or more times a day. Why is azithromycin still not the usual choice?","choices":["It is less effective against the usual conjunctivitis organisms","It cannot be used in contact lens wearers","It is considerably more expensive","It causes a white precipitate on the eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The dosing advantage is real and helps compliance, but cost keeps it from being used as often clinically. Erythromycin remains the common one. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic must be avoided outright in a patient with a sulfonamide allergy?","choices":["Trimethoprim with polymyxin B","Bacitracin","Sulfacetamide","Ciprofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is a sulfonamide, and it works by antagonising p-aminobenzoic acid to block folic acid synthesis. A documented sulfonamide allergy rules it out — one of the few genuine contraindications in the whole ophthalmic antibiotic list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why do gels, ointments and solid inserts increase the amount of drug absorbed compared with a simple solution?","choices":["They increase the drug's lipid solubility","They open tight junctions in the corneal epithelium","They reduce binding of drug to tear proteins","They prolong contact time in the cul-de-sac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. More time sitting in the cul-de-sac means more opportunity for transcorneal diffusion. Every formulation trick is aimed at the same variable — how long the drug stays in contact with the eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 6-7"},{"q":"A patient has adenoviral conjunctivitis. Which antiviral is indicated?","choices":["None is indicated","Topical trifluridine","Topical ganciclovir","Oral acyclovir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is no antiviral for viral conjunctivitis caused by adenoviruses. It runs its own course and you relieve the irritation. The ocular antivirals exist for keratitis, herpes zoster ophthalmicus and retinitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which risk factor for ophthalmic fungal infection is one a clinician can create?","choices":["Contact lens wear","Antecedent trauma","Topical corticosteroid use","Chronic ocular surface disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The risk list is trauma, chronic ocular surface disease, contact lens wear and immunosuppression — and topical steroid use is named explicitly as immunosuppression. It is the only one on the list you can introduce by prescribing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which non-infectious causes of conjunctivitis are commoner than the bacterial ones?","choices":["Only bacterial pathogens are listed as common","Tumours and systemic disease","Fungal keratitis and herpes zoster","Viruses, allergy, irritants and contact lenses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are given as the common causes, with immune-mediated reactions, systemic disease and tumours as less common. Bacterial pathogens come after — a reminder that most red eyes are not bacterial. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which agent is described as the most common ophthalmic antibiotic, and what property lets it be used before a bacterial cause is confirmed?","choices":["Azithromycin, because it is dosed less often","Ciprofloxacin, because it covers the widest spectrum","Erythromycin, because it is soothing on an inflamed eye","Bacitracin, because it has no drug interactions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the one you will see most, and because it soothes the inflamed eye it can reasonably be started even when a bacterial infection has not been confirmed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Two ophthalmic antibiotic classes both stop bacterial protein synthesis. What separates them?","choices":["Macrolides act at the 30S subunit, aminoglycosides at the 50S","Macrolides act at the 50S subunit, aminoglycosides at the 30S","Macrolides block transpeptidation, aminoglycosides block DNA gyrase","Both act at the 50S subunit but bind different sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same endpoint, different ribosomal subunit — the cleanest mechanism pairing among the ophthalmic antibiotics. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 15 and 23"},{"q":"Which delivery route carries the risk of globe perforation, optic nerve trauma and central retinal artery occlusion?","choices":["Topical instillation","Oral administration","Intravenous administration","Periocular injection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These periocular injections reach the anterior segment, posterior uveitis and cystoid macular oedema, but the needle is the hazard — perforation, optic nerve trauma, vascular occlusion and direct retinal drug toxicity if the globe is entered. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Amphotericin B appears on the ophthalmic antifungal table with more routes than any other agent. Which are they?","choices":["Topical solution and oral tablet only","Topical, subconjunctival, intravitreal, intravenous","Intravitreal and subconjunctival injection only","Topical suspension, and nothing else"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the one agent given by every route on the table, which is what makes it usable for endophthalmitis as well as surface disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A patient on long-term chloroquine develops a bull's eye lesion in the retina. Which pharmacokinetic property explains it?","choices":["Some drugs accumulate in ocular tissue after systemic absorption","Chloroquine is metabolised in the eye to a toxic product","Nasolacrimal drainage delivers a high local dose","Chloroquine binds tear proteins and is retained on the surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Distribution into the eye happens normally after systemic absorption, and a few drugs concentrate there over time. The bull's eye maculopathy is the named consequence. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Sulfacetamide and trimethoprim both interfere with folate, but at different points. What is the difference?","choices":["Sulfacetamide blocks reduction to tetrahydrofolate; trimethoprim antagonises p-aminobenzoic acid","Sulfacetamide inhibits the 30S ribosome; trimethoprim inhibits the 50S","Both block the same enzyme, so they are interchangeable","Sulfacetamide antagonises p-aminobenzoic acid; trimethoprim blocks reduction to tetrahydrofolate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sulfacetamide acts at the synthesis end by competing with p-aminobenzoic acid; trimethoprim acts one step later, stopping folic acid being reduced. Sequential blockade of the same pathway. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 18-19"},{"q":"Bacitracin ointment works on a structure none of the other ophthalmic antibiotics target. Which one?","choices":["The 50S ribosomal subunit","DNA gyrase","The bacterial cell wall","The cytoplasmic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacitracin stops mucopeptides being transferred into the growing wall. Every other ophthalmic antibiotic goes after the ribosome, DNA, folate or the membrane. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why has the microbiological spectrum of ocular infection shifted over time, by the example given?","choices":["Contact lens use increased Pseudomonas","Vaccination reduced Haemophilus influenzae","Fluoroquinolone use selected for resistant Staphylococcus","Topical steroid use increased fungal infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The spectrum is not fixed — the example given is the fall in Haemophilus influenzae after the vaccine was introduced, which changes what empiric therapy has to cover. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which factors determine the rate and extent of absorption of a topical ophthalmic drug?","choices":["Corneal blood flow and the rate of aqueous production","The patient's systemic renal and hepatic function","Intraocular pressure and pupil size","Contact time, drainage, protein binding and diffusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Four variables, and formulation changes act on the first two — which is why gels, ointments and inserts exist and why occluding the puncta increases absorption. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is notable about natamycin among the ophthalmic antifungals?","choices":["It is the only antifungal effective against Fusarium","It is the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal","It is the only antifungal given intravitreally","It is the only antifungal free of ocular irritation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Everything else on the antifungal table — amphotericin B, the imidazoles — is given by another route or has to be compounded. Availability is why natamycin is the one to know by name. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"When are cultures routinely taken before starting an ophthalmic antibiotic?","choices":["Not unless an unusual organism is expected","Always, because resistance is emerging","Only for conjunctivitis, not for keratitis","Only when the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum topical therapy is started empirically. Cultures are reserved for when something unusual is likely — the immunocompromised patient is the example given. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Erythromycin ointment and azithromycin solution share a mechanism. What is it?","choices":["Inhibition of protein synthesis at the 50S ribosomal subunit","Inhibition of protein synthesis at the 30S ribosomal subunit","Inhibition of DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Inhibition of cell wall mucopeptide transfer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The macrolides block transpeptidation at the 50S subunit, so RNA-dependent protein synthesis stops and the organism dies. Knowing the subunit is what separates them from the aminoglycosides. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
